            'visa_subclass_code' => 'Schengen Type C',
            'intro_html' => '<p>Liechtenstein\'s Schengen-area status is confirmed, not assumed: it joined the European Economic Area in 1995 (following EFTA membership in 1991), signed the Schengen Association Agreement on 28 February 2008, and became an operational member of the Schengen Area on 19 December 2011, alongside Switzerland (per WebSearch snippets corroborated by the Dutch government\'s official EU/EEA/EFTA/Schengen country list and historical reporting on the 2011 Schengen enlargement). Liechtenstein has no diplomatic mission of its own in India and no international airport; Switzerland represents it for Schengen visa purposes, so Indian applicants apply through the Embassy of Switzerland in New Delhi or its outsourced partner VFS Global, using the standard Schengen short-stay (Type C) visa.</p><p>There is no separate "Liechtenstein sports visa." However, Swiss diplomatic missions (which also cover Liechtenstein) publish a specific "Visa for Sport Events" supporting-document checklist as a recognised sub-category of the ordinary Schengen Type C visa — one such checklist found in this research (issued via the Swiss mission network, e.g. the Kenya-market version) explicitly states it covers travel to Liechtenstein as well as to Switzerland. This is a documentation pathway within the single Schengen visa, not a distinct visa type.</p>',
