            'visa_subclass_code' => null,
            'intro_html' => '<p>Madagascar is one of the few countries in this batch where a specifically-named sports-related visa category could be documented, though the evidence comes from Madagascar\'s own diplomatic missions abroad rather than a single central government portal, and the details vary slightly between missions. Visa-requirement sheets published by the Embassy of Madagascar in Berlin and the Embassy of Madagascar in London (per WebSearch snippets from ambamad-berlin.diplomatie.gov.mg and mdg-london.org) both list a "Sport and Culture" visa as a distinct short-term visa category, separate from the ordinary tourist, business and conference visas those same missions also list.</p><p>The Berlin mission\'s sheet describes this as a short-term visa that can be extended up to 90 days but cannot be converted into a long-stay visa, and lists a fairly demanding document set (passport, photo, return-ticket proof, fee payment, a certificate of residence and a police-clearance certificate each less than three months old, a yellow fever certificate, and a letter of invitation). The London mission\'s sheet, for the same up-to-90-day short-term visa, is less demanding and states that sport events require no additional documents beyond the standard short-term-visa list, with a fee of GBP 30 for a multiple-entry 90-day visa. These two official-mission sources disagree on how demanding the sport-visa paperwork actually is, so applicants should confirm the exact list with the Madagascar mission serving their own location (for India, likely the Embassy of Madagascar in New Delhi, which was not directly reached in this research) rather than assuming either sheet applies as-is.</p><p>Separately, several commercial/aggregator sources describe an eVisa (for tourism, commonly 15 or 30 days single entry) or a visa-on-arrival (commonly cited around 60 days) for Indian citizens — but these are general tourist routes, distinct from the named "Sport and Culture" visa, and appear intended for simple leisure travel rather than organised sporting participation.</p>',
