            'eligibility_html' => '<p>Indian athletes, coaches, officials and cultural/sports delegations travelling to Madagascar specifically to participate in or attend a sporting event can apply for the "Sport and Culture" visa documented by Madagascar\'s diplomatic missions abroad. Based on the Berlin and London mission sheets located in this research, the core requirements include a passport (commonly required to be valid at least six months beyond arrival), a passport photo, proof of a return ticket or booking, payment of the visa fee, a yellow fever vaccination certificate, and a letter of invitation; the Berlin sheet additionally lists a certificate of residence and a police-clearance certificate (both less than three months old), while the London sheet does not require these extra documents for sport events. This discrepancy between two official-mission sources means the exact list should be confirmed with the Madagascar mission handling the Indian applicant\'s case.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Amateur vs Paid/Professional Engagement</h4><p>The "Sport and Culture" visa is a short-term visitor category and, per the Berlin sheet, cannot be converted into a long-stay visa — consistent with amateur participation or attending/competing in an event rather than a paid, contracted engagement. Whether a professional/contracted sportsperson would instead need a separate work-authorised route was not independently confirmed in this research.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>General Tourist/eVisa Route</h4><p>For simple attendance as a spectator rather than organised participation, Indian travellers may alternatively be able to use Madagascar\'s general eVisa or visa-on-arrival tourist routes — though sources conflict on the stay length (15/30 days for the eVisa versus around 60 days for visa-on-arrival), so this should be confirmed directly before relying on it.</p>',
            'indian_applicant_html' => '<h4 style=\'margin:0 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Competing Athletes, Coaches and Officials</h4><p>Apply for the "Sport and Culture" visa through the Madagascar diplomatic mission serving your location (for Indian applicants, this would normally be the Embassy of Madagascar handling India, which was not directly confirmed in this research), using a letter of invitation describing the event, your passport, photo, return-ticket proof, yellow fever certificate and the visa fee. Check with that specific mission whether it also requires a police-clearance certificate and residence certificate, since the Berlin and London mission sheets disagree on this point.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Spectators/Casual Travel</h4><p>If you are travelling only to watch rather than participate or officiate, the general eVisa or visa-on-arrival tourist route may be simpler — our consultants can help confirm which route and stay length currently applies.</p>',
