            'eligibility_html' => '<p>No dedicated sports or athlete visa category for Montenegro was located, and a 2026 sports-event page itself points competitors to Montenegro\'s general visa-information page rather than any sport-specific process. On the evidence found, Indian athletes, coaches, officials or sports delegations travelling to Montenegro would use the general entry-visa regime, which is itself described two different ways across sources: either a standard visa obtained in advance from a Montenegrin embassy/consulate/visa centre, or — per a separate guide — a conditional visa-free allowance of up to 30 days available only to those already holding a valid Schengen (Type C/D), US, UK or Irish visa or residence permit.</p><h4 style=\'margin:20px 0 10px;font-size:16px;\'>Amateur vs Paid/Professional Engagement</h4><p>Because no sports-specific category exists in the evidence found, the amateur/professional distinction cannot be confirmed for Montenegro specifically. As a general principle, a standard entry/visitor-type visa would not be expected to authorise paid employment; a contracted/paid sporting engagement would be expected to need separate work authorisation, but this has not been independently confirmed for Montenegro.</p>',
